phd=dict(id='Phd_CE_1405_Q18',booklet='Phd_CE_1405',pdfPage=5,questionNumber=18,topics=['a_arrays','l_matrices'],title='A matrix representation for row and column operations',stem=r'The outer product of two vectors $A=(a_0,\ldots,a_{n-1})$ and $B=(b_0,\ldots,b_{n-1})$ defines $C_{ij}=a_i b_j$. Which listed representation is most suitable when C must be stored in correspondence with the vectors at arbitrary dimension n and matrix addition and multiplication should be straightforward?',options=['A circular singly linked list with a head node whose heads are also linked in a list.','An orthogonal linked list.','A circular singly linked list with a head node.','A circular singly linked list.'],answer=2,solution=r'The question asks for a matrix representation supporting matrix operations, not merely evaluating one entry of a rank-one formula. Orthogonal links organize every represented entry in both its row and column, providing the two traversal directions needed to combine corresponding rows and columns. A row-list hierarchy organizes rows but lacks an explicit column chain. The one-chain choices organize only one successor relation. Thus option 2 is the most suitable listed general matrix representation. Factorized storage of A and B can be smaller for the single outer product, but arbitrary addition is not closed within one outer product and factorization is not among the listed choices. This is a suitability judgment under the question’s general-operation requirement, not a claim that no other representation can perform matrix arithmetic. The answer is independently reasoned; no official key was available.',repoPath=path,sourceSha256=hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest(),sourceCommit='bdadf6e2c9cadc4772ae137a96a3da753c7cfd08',translationStatus='Checked against original rendered PDF page 5',answerProvenance='Independent derivation; not an official key',origin='Authentic examination adaptation')
